<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.12.5: Identifying Graphs of Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01941</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.12.5: Identifying Graphs of Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1c0dee12-9b8e-4461-8dd7-2b869c39b9fc</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title> Launch </title>
<para>Since this activity was designed to be completed without technology, ask students to put away any devices.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Here are graphs that represent three quadratic functions, defined by:</para>
<para>\(f(x)=x^2-4\)
  \(g(x)=1-x^2\)
  \(h(x)=x^2+4\)</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Coordinate plane with 3 quadratic functions. Curve A opens up and has minimum at 0 comma 4. Curve B opens up and has minimum at negative 4 comma 0. Curve C opens down and has maximum 0 comma 1."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1315d2846792f9c61cb68b722d2e7a89c489119b"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which graph represents the equation \(f(x)=x^2-4\)?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Green Graph A </item>
  <item> Blue Graph B </item>
  <item> Black Graph C </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Blue Graph B</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which graph represents the equation \(g(x)=1-x^2\)?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Green Graph A </item>
  <item> Blue Graph B </item>
  <item> Black Graph C </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Black Graph C</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which graph represents the equation \(h(x)=x^2+4\)?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> Green Graph A </item>
  <item> Blue Graph B </item>
  <item> Black Graph C </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Green Graph A</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which part of the equation tells us that Graph C opens downward?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=1-x^2\) is the equation. The negative coefficient of \(x^2\) makes the graph open downward.</para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>Press Pause</title>
<para>If students are still struggling with finding \(x\)- and \(y\)-intercepts of the graph, consider skipping lesson 7.13 and, instead, addressing misconceptions in the cool downs and working on practice problems.</para></section>
</content>
